questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where a distributed saga step implemented in a Spring Boot microservice fails to roll back database changes when a downstream invocation fails. The method snippet is as follows:

java
@Service
public class PaymentSagaService {

    @Autowired
    private AccountRepository accountRepository;

    @Autowired
    private PaymentClient paymentClient;

    @Transactional
    public void processPayment(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) throws PaymentProcessingException {
        accountRepository.reserveFunds(accountId, amount);
        try {
            paymentClient.executeRemotePayment(accountId, amount);
        } catch (FeignException e) {
            throw new PaymentProcessingException("Remote payment failed", e);
        }
    }
}


When `FeignException` is caught and rethrown as `PaymentProcessingException` (which extends `java.lang.Exception`), the `accountRepository.reserveFunds` transaction commits successfully instead of rolling back.

You are debugging a production issue where an asynchronous distributed worker microservice uses `Resilience4j` `@CircuitBreaker` on an asynchronous method returning `CompletableFuture<String>`. Under heavy downstream latency and HTTP 500 responses, the circuit breaker remains in the `CLOSED` state and fails to transition to `OPEN`, allowing cascading failures to overwhelm the system.

The method signature is:

java
@CircuitBreaker(name = "downstreamService")
public CompletableFuture<String> callDownstreamAsync() {
    return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> restTemplate.getForObject(URL, String.class));
}
